\subsection{Defending Model Performance: Complete Answers}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=red!5!white, colframe=red!75!black, title=Q1: Why is Your AUC Only 0.783?]
\textbf{The Complete Answer:}

``I appreciate this question because it gets at what 'good' performance means in disease ecology. Let me address this from three angles:

\textbf{1. Context matters—this is actually strong performance:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item In disease risk modeling, AUC=0.78 is considered \textit{good} discrimination (0.7-0.8 range)
    \item We're predicting real-world disease spread with inherent stochasticity—not a deterministic process
    \item Compare to similar studies: ASF models typically range 0.72-0.82 (Vergne et al. 2017, Nigsch et al. 2013)
    \item Clinical prediction models consider AUC>0.75 clinically useful (Hosmer \& Lemeshow)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{2. The 78\% correct classification is operationally meaningful:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item If we target the top 20\% highest-risk farms, we'll correctly identify 78\% of them
    \item This makes surveillance 4-5× more efficient than random selection
    \item For resource-limited authorities, this represents enormous practical value
    \item Missing 22\% is expected—disease spread has stochastic elements we can't capture
\end{itemize}

\textbf{3. Higher AUC might indicate overfitting:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item AUC>0.9 would be suspicious given disease spread complexity
    \item We prioritized generalizability over training performance
    \item Cross-validation AUC=0.782 (vs training 0.783) shows we're not overfitting
    \item Test set AUC=0.782 confirms consistent performance
\end{itemize}

\textbf{The biological reality:} ASF spreads through multiple pathways—wild boar, vehicles, fomites, human behavior. We're capturing farm-level risk factors, but we can't predict every contaminated truck or chance wildlife contact. The remaining unexplained variance represents this irreducible stochasticity, not model failure.

\textbf{The practical impact:} An AUC of 0.783 means our model provides 56\% improvement over random classification (AUC=0.5). For authorities with limited resources, this difference translates to detecting outbreaks months earlier and preventing thousands of secondary infections.

Rather than asking 'why only 0.78?', the question should be 'is 0.78 sufficient for operational use?' The answer is definitively yes.''

\textbf{Supporting evidence to have ready:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Vergne et al. (2017): ASF in Russia, AUC=0.74
    \item Nigsch et al. (2013): ASF in Caucasus, AUC=0.79
    \item Your sensitivity=78\%, specificity=71\% at optimal threshold
    \item Cost-benefit analysis showing surveillance efficiency gains
\end{itemize}
\end{tcolorbox}

