\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=yellow!5!white, colframe=yellow!75!black, title=Q3: What About Spatial Autocorrelation?]
\textbf{The Complete Answer:}

``This is an excellent methodological question. Spatial autocorrelation in disease data is expected and presents both challenges and opportunities. Here's how I addressed it:

\textbf{1. Yes, Spatial Autocorrelation Exists—And It Should:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Moran's I for outbreak occurrence: 0.34 (p<0.001)
    \item This is \textit{expected}—diseases spread through space via transmission
    \item Clustering reflects real epidemiological processes, not just statistical artifact
    \item The question isn't whether SAC exists, but whether we've \textit{accounted} for it
\end{itemize}

\textbf{2. Spatial Predictors Capture Transmission Processes:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Mean susceptible pigs within 3 km explicitly models spatial transmission risk
    \item This variable accounts for local clustering by quantifying exposure
    \item After including spatial predictors, residual SAC is minimal
    \item Moran's I on model residuals: 0.09 (p=0.06)—no significant residual clustering
\end{itemize}

\textbf{3. Model Structure Addresses SAC:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item We're not claiming farms are independent observations
    \item Logistic regression with spatial covariates is appropriate for SAC
    \item Standard errors adjusted for potential clustering (robust SEs)
    \item Cross-validation by county tests spatial generalizability
\end{itemize}

\textbf{4. Why Not Spatial Regression Models (SAR/CAR)?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Considered spatial lag and spatial error models
    \item These models are better for \textit{residual} SAC after accounting for predictors
    \item Our spatial covariates already capture the biological mechanism
    \item Spatial random effects would complicate interpretation without improving prediction
    \item AUC with spatial lag model: 0.785 (negligible 0.002 improvement)
    \item Chose parsimony and interpretability
\end{itemize}

\textbf{5. Biological Interpretation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item SAC in raw data = disease spreads locally (biology)
    \item We model \textit{why} it spreads locally (susceptible pig density)
    \item This is more informative than just adding spatial smoothing
    \item We want to know \textit{what} drives clustering, not just \textit{that} it clusters
\end{itemize}

\textbf{6. Implications for Inference:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item P-values and CIs account for potential dependence (robust SEs)
    \item Spatial CV confirms predictions generalize to new regions
    \item SAC doesn't invalidate our risk factors—they're still real effects
    \item Prediction accuracy (AUC) is unbiased even with SAC
\end{itemize}

\textbf{The key insight:} Spatial autocorrelation isn't a problem to eliminate—it's a biological signal to model. By including spatial transmission predictors (susceptible pig density), we've incorporated the mechanism causing SAC. The minimal residual clustering suggests we've successfully captured spatial processes.

\textbf{Alternative approach considered:} We could add county random effects, but this would:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Reduce interpretability (what does a random effect mean biologically?)
    \item Complicate predictions (need county-specific estimates)
    \item Only marginally improve fit
    \item Obscure the mechanistic drivers we're trying to identify
\end{itemize}

Our approach prioritizes biological mechanism over statistical correction.''
\end{tcolorbox}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=teal!5!white, colframe=teal!75!black, title=Q4: Can You Claim Causality?]
\textbf{The Complete Answer:}

``This is a critical distinction, and I'm careful about causal language. Let me be precise about what we can and cannot conclude:

\textbf{What We CAN Say:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item These variables are \textit{associated} with increased outbreak risk
    \item Farms with high crop diversity have higher outbreak odds (OR=1.53)
    \item These are \textit{predictive} risk factors useful for surveillance
    \item The associations are strong, consistent, and biologically plausible
    \item We've controlled for confounding through multivariate modeling
\end{itemize}

\textbf{What We CANNOT Say:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item We cannot claim crop diversity \textit{causes} outbreaks
    \item We haven't established temporal precedence (predictors measured at same time as outbreaks)
    \item We can't rule out unmeasured confounding
    \item This is observational, not experimental data
    \item Correlation ≠ causation, even with strong associations
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Hill's Criteria for Causation—Where We Stand:}

\textbf{Strength:} ✓ Strong associations (OR 1.44-1.95)

\textbf{Consistency:} ✓ Aligns with previous ASF studies (Vergne, Boklund)

\textbf{Biological Plausibility:} ✓ Clear mechanisms:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Susceptible pig density → local transmission
    \item Crop diversity → more farm visitors, equipment sharing
    \item Feed crops → vehicle/wildlife exposure
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Temporal Sequence:} ✗ Cross-sectional design—predictors and outcomes measured together

\textbf{Dose-Response:} ✓ Higher exposure = higher risk (linear relationship with susceptible pigs)

\textbf{Experimental Evidence:} ✗ Cannot experimentally manipulate farm characteristics

\textbf{The Honest Assessment:} We have strong \textit{evidence for} causation but not \textit{proof} of causation. This is typical for observational epidemiology. The associations are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Strong enough to inform policy
    \item Biologically plausible enough to be credible
    \item Consistent enough to be reliable
    \item But not proven enough to claim definitive causation
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Potential Alternative Explanations:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Crop diversity might proxy for farm complexity or management quality
    \item Feed crop production might correlate with unmeasured biosecurity
    \item Susceptible pig density might reflect regional factors we haven't captured
    \item Confounding by farm size, region, or management practices
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Why This Still Matters for Policy:}

Public health acts on associations all the time:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Smoking and lung cancer (long observational before mechanism proven)
    \item Seat belts and injury reduction (observational evidence drove policy)
    \item Vaccine effectiveness (observational surveillance informs recommendations)
\end{itemize}

We don't need perfect causal proof to make better surveillance decisions. We need:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Strong, consistent associations ✓
    \item Biological plausibility ✓
    \item Better predictions than current practice ✓
    \item Acknowledgment of limitations ✓
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Future Work for Causal Inference:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Longitudinal data with temporal lags
    \item Intervention studies (targeted biosecurity improvements)
    \item Natural experiments (policy changes affecting farm characteristics)
    \item Causal inference methods (propensity scores, instrumental variables)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{The Bottom Line:} I use terms like ``associated with,'' ``predictive of,'' and ``risk factor'' rather than ``causes'' or ``results in.'' This is scientifically honest and still provides actionable information for disease control.''
\end{tcolorbox}

