\textbf{4. Generalizability Concerns:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ML models often overfit despite cross-validation
    \item Spatial CV with RF: AUC drops to 0.761 (vs 0.779 for logistic)
    \item Logistic regression more stable across geographic regions
    \item For real-world application, robust generalization > marginal training gains
\end{itemize}

\textbf{5. Biological Relationships are Likely Linear:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Disease risk scales approximately linearly with exposure
    \item No evidence of complex interactions or thresholds
    \item Checked for non-linearity: splines showed linear relationships
    \item Random forest's flexibility isn't needed—it fits noise
\end{itemize}

\textbf{6. Transparency and Trust:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Stakeholders can audit logistic regression
    \item Coefficients are reproducible and verifiable
    \item Authorities can implement in Excel/simple software
    \item RF requires specialized software and expertise
    \item Trust is essential for policy adoption
\end{itemize}

\textbf{7. Practical Implementation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Logistic regression: calculate risk score = β₀ + β₁X₁ + β₂X₂...
    \item Can be programmed in any system
    \item RF: need entire ensemble, complex infrastructure
    \item Simpler models are more likely to be actually used
\end{itemize}

\textbf{8. Variable Importance vs. Effect Sizes:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item RF provides importance rankings but not magnitudes
    \item How much does crop diversity increase risk? RF can't say directly
    \item Logistic regression: OR=1.53 (53\% increase)—quantifiable
    \item Effect sizes inform intervention design
\end{itemize}

\textbf{When Would I Choose Random Forest?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item If performance difference was >5\% AUC
    \item If interpretability wasn't required
    \item If we had 100+ predictors (high dimensionality)
    \item If exploratory variable discovery was the goal
    \item If non-linear relationships were evident
\end{itemize}

\textbf{The Decision Framework I Used:}

\textbf{Question 1:} Does ML meaningfully outperform logistic regression?
\begin{itemize}
    \item No—0.8\% difference, not significant
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Question 2:} Is interpretability important?
\begin{itemize}
    \item Yes—policy requires understanding mechanisms
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Question 3:} Are relationships likely non-linear/interactive?
\begin{itemize}
    \item No—biological theory suggests linear effects
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Decision:} Logistic regression is the appropriate choice.

\textbf{The Broader Point:} Machine learning is not inherently superior. Model choice should be driven by:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Research questions (explanation vs. prediction)
    \item Performance trade-offs (marginal gains vs. interpretability loss)
    \item Practical constraints (implementation, stakeholder trust)
    \item Data characteristics (dimensionality, sample size, signal strength)
\end{enumerate}

For disease surveillance policy, logistic regression's transparency and equivalent performance make it the optimal choice.''
\end{tcolorbox}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=magenta!5!white, colframe=magenta!75!black, title=Q6: Why Only 18\% Explained Variance (Pseudo R²)?]
\textbf{The Complete Answer:}

``This question reflects a common misunderstanding about R² in logistic regression and disease modeling. Let me clarify:

\textbf{1. Pseudo R² ≠ Linear Regression R²:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Linear R²: proportion of variance in continuous outcome
    \item Pseudo R²: approximation of model fit for binary outcome
    \item These are \textit{not comparable}—different scales and interpretations
    \item McFadden's R²=0.18 is considered \textit{excellent fit} (McFadden, 1979)
    \item Guidelines: R²>0.2 = good fit, >0.4 = very good (Domencich \& McFadden)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{2. What Does R²=0.184 Actually Mean?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Model improvement over null (intercept-only) model
    \item Log-likelihood ratio: 18.4\% improvement in fit
    \item This is \textit{substantial} for binary outcomes
    \item Comparable published studies: 0.12-0.22 range
    \item We're on the higher end of typical performance
\end{itemize}

\textbf{3. AUC is the Better Metric for Binary Outcomes:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item AUC=0.783 means 78\% discrimination ability
    \item This is more interpretable for classification tasks
    \item R² measures fit, AUC measures discrimination—different constructs
    \item For disease surveillance (classification), AUC matters more
    \item Our AUC is strong; R² confirms good model fit
\end{itemize}

\textbf{4. Why Not Higher R²?—Biological Reality:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Disease spread is inherently stochastic
    \item We're modeling probability, not deterministic outcomes
    \item Multiple unmeasured transmission routes:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Wild boar movements (unpredictable)
        \item Contaminated vehicles (hard to track)
        \item Human behavior (highly variable)
        \item Fomites (unobserved)
    \end{itemize}
    \item Perfect prediction (R²=1.0) would be suspicious—suggests overfitting
    \item The ``unexplained'' 82\% includes true randomness, not just missing variables
\end{itemize}

\textbf{5. Comparison to Other Disease Models:}

\textbf{Published ASF Studies:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Vergne et al. (2017): Pseudo R²=0.16
    \item Boklund et al. (2020): Pseudo R²=0.19
    \item Andraud et al. (2019): Pseudo R²=0.14
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Other Animal Diseases:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Avian influenza models: R²=0.10-0.18
    \item Foot-and-mouth disease: R²=0.15-0.23
    \item Bovine tuberculosis: R²=0.12-0.20
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Our R²=0.184 is solidly within the expected range—actually toward the high end.}

\textbf{6. What Would Higher R² Require?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Detailed movement data (truck records, visitor logs)—unavailable
    \item Wildlife surveillance (wild boar tracking)—cost-prohibitive
    \item Real-time biosecurity practices (compliance monitoring)—not recorded
    \item Individual animal data (pig-level exposure)—privacy/scale issues
    \item Even with all this, stochastic transmission limits predictability
\end{itemize}

\textbf{7. The Prediction Paradox:}

\textbf{If R²=0.8+:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Would suggest near-deterministic process
    \item ASF spread is not deterministic
    \item High R² would indicate overfitting or data leakage
    \item I would be suspicious of the model
\end{itemize}

\textbf{R²=0.18 actually suggests:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item We've captured major systematic risk factors
    \item We haven't overfit to noise
    \item Model reflects biological reality (stochastic spread)
    \item Predictions are honest about uncertainty
\end{itemize}

\textbf{8. Practical Implications:}

\textbf{The question isn't ``why only 18\%?'' but ``is 18\% enough to improve surveillance?''}

\textbf{Answer: Yes.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item 18\% explained variance = 56\% improvement over random (AUC 0.5→0.78)
    \item Targeting top 20\% of farms captures 44\% of outbreaks (vs. 20\% by chance)
    \item This makes surveillance 2.2× more efficient
    \item For resource-limited authorities, this is transformative
\end{itemize}

\textbf{9. Alternative Metrics Tell Consistent Story:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item AIC: 8,945 (vs null model: 11,023)—substantial improvement
    \item BIC: 9,067 (vs null model: 11,035)—favors our model
    \item Deviance reduction: 2,078 (χ²=2078, p<0.001)—highly significant
    \item All metrics confirm strong model fit
\end{itemize}

\textbf{10. The Epistemological Point:}

In ecology and epidemiology, low R² doesn't mean:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Bad model
    \item Weak predictors
    \item Missing important variables
\end{itemize}

It often means:
\begin{itemize}
    \item High biological variability (true, not measurement error)
    \item Stochastic processes dominate
    \item Multiple causal pathways exist
    \item Prediction has inherent limits
\end{itemize}

\textbf{The Bottom Line:} Pseudo R²=0.18 represents \textit{strong model fit} for disease risk modeling. Comparing it to linear regression R² is inappropriate. Our discrimination ability (AUC=0.78) is the relevant metric, and it demonstrates operationally meaningful prediction. The moderate R² reflects honest modeling of a stochastic process, not model inadequacy.''
\end{tcolorbox}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=lime!5!white, colframe=lime!75!black, title=Q7: What About Multicollinearity?]
\textbf{The Complete Answer:}

``Multicollinearity was carefully assessed, and I took specific steps to address it. Here's the complete analysis:

\textbf{1. Formal Assessment—Variance Inflation Factors (VIF):}

\textbf{Results:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Crop diversity: VIF=10.3 (flagged)
    \item Feed crops: VIF=8.9
    \item Mean susceptible pigs: VIF=4.2
    \item Herd size: VIF=3.8
    \item All other predictors: VIF<3.0
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Interpretation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item VIF<5: no concern
    \item VIF 5-10: moderate concern, monitor
    \item VIF>10: serious concern, action needed
\end{itemize}

\textbf{One variable (crop diversity) exceeds threshold marginally.}

\textbf{2. Why Crop Diversity VIF=10.3?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Correlation with feed crops: r=0.71
    \item Biological reason: farms producing feed often have diverse rotations
    \item This is expected, not problematic—they measure related but distinct concepts
    \item Crop diversity = landscape heterogeneity (5+ crop types)
    \item Feed crops = specific production activity (maize, wheat for animal feed)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{3. Does This Multicollinearity Matter?}

\textbf{For Prediction: NO}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Multicollinearity doesn't bias predictions
    \item It affects coefficient stability, not predictive accuracy
    \item Our AUC=0.783 is unaffected by VIF
    \item Cross-validation confirms strong prediction
\end{itemize}

\textbf{For Inference: Minimal Impact}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Coefficients remain significant (p<0.001 for both)
    \item Confidence intervals are wider but still exclude 1.0
    \item Crop diversity OR: 1.53 [95\% CI: 1.41-1.66]
    \item Feed crops OR: 1.44 [95\% CI: 1.33-1.57]
    \item CIs don't overlap with null, so effects are robust
\end{itemize}

\textbf{4. Actions Taken to Address Multicollinearity:}

\textbf{Step 1: Correlation Matrix Screening}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Examined all pairwise correlations before modeling
    \item Removed redundant variables: pig density removed (r=0.89 with herd size)
    \item Kept variables with r<0.8 when biologically distinct
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 2: Biological Justification}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Each variable represents different mechanism:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Crop diversity → visitor frequency, equipment sharing
        \item Feed crops → vehicle traffic, wildlife attraction
    \end{itemize}
    \item Not mathematically redundant even if correlated
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 3: Sensitivity Analysis}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Model without crop diversity: AUC=0.769 (drops 0.014)
    \item Model without feed crops: AUC=0.775 (drops 0.008)
    \item Both contribute unique predictive information
    \item Kept both because they improve prediction
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step 4: Ridge Regression Comparison}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Ridge regression penalizes collinear coefficients
    \item Ridge AUC: 0.781 (vs logistic 0.783)
    \item Ridge coefficients: crop diversity=0.38, feed crops=0.32
    \item Logistic coefficients: crop diversity=0.42, feed crops=0.37
    \item Minimal difference—multicollinearity not inflating estimates
\end{itemize}

\textbf{5. Why I Kept Both Variables Despite VIF=10.3:}

\textbf{Justification 1: Distinct Mechanisms}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Removing either would lose biological information
    \item They're correlated but not redundant
    \item Both have independent effects on risk
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Justification 2: Stable Estimates}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Bootstrap SEs show coefficient stability
    \item 1000 bootstrap iterations: CV<0.15 for both coefficients
    \item Estimates are reliable despite correlation
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Justification 3: Both Statistically Significant}
\begin{itemize}
    \item If multicollinearity was severe, one would become non-significant
    \item Both p<0.001 in full model
    \item Both CIs exclude null
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Justification 4: Better Prediction}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Full model outperforms reduced models
    \item We prioritize prediction over perfectly uncorrelated predictors
\end{itemize}

\textbf{6. What Would I Do Differently If This Were a Problem?}

\textbf{If VIF>20 or coefficients unstable:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Create composite variable (crop production intensity index)
    \item Use principal components analysis
    \item Drop one variable based on biological priority
    \item Use penalized regression (lasso/ridge)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{But with VIF=10.3 and stable estimates, these steps are unnecessary.}

\textbf{7. Common Misconceptions About Multicollinearity:}

\textbf{Myth 1:} Any correlation between predictors is bad.
\begin{itemize}
    \item False—moderate correlations are expected and fine
    \item Problem only when correlations are extreme (r>0.9)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Myth 2:} VIF>10 means remove the variable immediately.
\begin{itemize}
    \item False—VIF=10 is a guideline, not a rule
    \item Consider: stability, significance, biological meaning, prediction
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Myth 3:} Multicollinearity biases predictions.
\begin{itemize}
    \item False—predictions are unbiased
    \item Only coefficient interpretation is affected
\end{itemize}

\textbf{8. Evidence Multicollinearity is Not a Problem Here:}

✓ Cross-validation performance stable
✓ Test set performance matches training
✓ Bootstrap CIs are narrow
✓ Coefficients remain significant
✓ Sign and magnitude are biologically sensible
✓ Ridge regression yields similar estimates
✓ Prediction accuracy is strong

\textbf{The Verdict:} Multicollinearity is present at moderate levels (expected given biological relationships) but does not compromise model validity, interpretation, or predictions. I monitored it, assessed its impact, and determined that retaining correlated predictors provides better biological insight and prediction than enforcing arbitrary VIF thresholds.''
\end{tcolorbox}

