\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=brown!5!white, colframe=brown!75!black, title=Q9: What If You're Wrong About a Predictor?]
\textbf{The Complete, Honest Answer:}

``This is an excellent question that gets at uncertainty and humility in science. Let me be direct: I \textit{could} be wrong, and here's how I think about that:

\textbf{1. ACKNOWLEDGING UNCERTAINTY}

\textbf{What We Don't Know:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item True causal mechanisms (observational data, not experimental)
    \item Unmeasured confounders (biosecurity practices, compliance, farm management quality)
    \item Temporal dynamics (cross-sectional snapshot, not longitudinal)
    \item Individual-level behavior (farmer decisions, visitor protocols)
    \item Wild boar movements (major transmission pathway, poorly measured)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Sources of Potential Error:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Misclassification: Farm characteristics may be measured with error
    \item Confounding: Crop diversity might proxy for something else (farm complexity, region)
    \item Reverse causation: Outbreak history might change farm behavior
    \item Selection bias: Commercial farms only, missing backyard operations
    \item Spatial confounding: Regional policies or practices we haven't captured
\end{itemize}

\textbf{2. SPECIFIC PREDICTOR CONCERNS}

\textbf{Crop Diversity (OR=1.53):}

\textbf{If I'm wrong, alternative explanations:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Proxies for farm complexity or management capacity
    \item Correlates with regional agricultural intensification
    \item Reflects data quality issues (more diverse farms better reported)
    \item Confounded by farm size or economic status
\end{itemize}

\textbf{How I'd detect this:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Longitudinal analysis: does changing crop diversity change risk?
    \item Farm-level intervention: biosecurity improvements on diverse farms
    \item Detailed confounding control: add management quality measures
    \item Causal inference methods: propensity score matching, IV analysis
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Feed Crops (OR=1.44):}

\textbf{If I'm wrong, alternative explanations:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Correlates with intensive farming regions (regional effect, not farm effect)
    \item Proxies for economic status or modernization
    \item Reflects wildlife habitat overlap (not feed crops per se)
    \item Measurement error (feed crop definition ambiguous)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Mean Susceptible Pigs (OR=1.95):}

\textbf{If I'm wrong (less likely), alternative explanations:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Spatial confounding: high-density regions differ systematically
    \item Detection bias: more pigs = more surveillance
    \item Social factors: high-density regions have different practices
\end{itemize}

\textbf{This predictor has strongest evidence—most likely to be real effect.}

\textbf{3. SAFEGUARDS AGAINST BEING WRONG}

\textbf{Built-in Protections:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Multiple variable selection methods (reduces data-mining false positives)
    \item Biological plausibility requirement (theory-grounded)
    \item Cross-validation (tests generalization)
    \item Consistency with published literature (not idiosyncratic to our data)
    \item Conservative statistical thresholds (p<0.05, not p<0.10)
    \item Multicollinearity checks (avoids spurious associations)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Red Flags I Watched For:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Implausible effect sizes (OR>5 would be suspicious)
    \item Sign reversals across models (instability)
    \item Sensitivity to single observations (influence diagnostics)
    \item Non-robustness to specification (tested multiple model forms)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{4. WHAT WOULD HAPPEN IF I'M WRONG?}

\textbf{Scenario A: Crop Diversity is Spurious}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Model would still work for prediction (correlation still exists)
    \item But mechanism would be misunderstood
    \item Interventions targeting crop diversity wouldn't work
    \item Surveillance targeting would still be useful (correlated with true risk)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Impact:} Moderate—prediction still valid, but explanation wrong.

\textbf{Scenario B: Feed Crops is Confounded}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Similar to Scenario A
    \item Variable still predictive, even if not causal
    \item Policy would still identify high-risk farms
    \item But targeted interventions might miss true mechanism
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Impact:} Moderate—risk scores still useful, intervention design affected.

\textbf{Scenario C: Susceptible Pigs is Wrong (unlikely)}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Would fundamentally undermine model
    \item But extensive literature supports transmission pressure
    \item This is the least likely to be wrong
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Impact:} Severe—but very low probability.

\textbf{5. HOW I'D FIND OUT I'M WRONG}

\textbf{Future Validation Opportunities:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Prospective validation: apply model to new outbreaks 2023+
    \item External validation: test in other countries (Poland, Hungary)
    \item Intervention studies: improve biosecurity on predicted high-risk farms
    \item Longitudinal data: track farms over time, test temporal associations
    \item Mechanistic studies: detailed farm-level investigations of transmission
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Warning Signs to Watch:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Model performance degrades over time (changing epidemiology)
    \item Predictors stop being significant in new data
    \item Interventions based on model don't reduce risk
    \item Alternative explanations gain empirical support
\end{itemize}

\textbf{6. THE SCIENTIFIC PROCESS}

\textbf{Current Evidence Status:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Strong support:} Mean susceptible pigs (mechanistic, consistent, large effect)
    \item \textbf{Moderate support:} Feed crops, crop diversity (plausible, consistent, needs validation)
    \item \textbf{Exploratory:} Some landscape variables (weaker effects, less theory)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Not All Predictors Are Equal:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item I'm most confident in transmission pressure (susceptible pigs)
    \item Moderately confident in farm activity variables (crops, feed)
    \item Least confident in some landscape features
    \item This hierarchy informs policy—prioritize high-confidence predictors
\end{itemize}

\textbf{7. IMPLICATIONS FOR POLICY}

\textbf{Despite Uncertainty, We Should Act Because:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Current approach (untargeted surveillance) is clearly suboptimal
    \item Model-based targeting is better than random, even if imperfect
    \item We can update model as new evidence emerges
    \item Precautionary principle: prevent outbreaks while learning
    \item Alternative (do nothing) has known high costs
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Adaptive Management Strategy:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Implement model-based surveillance
    \item Monitor performance prospectively
    \item Collect new data (farm practices, biosecurity)
    \item Update model annually with new outbreaks
    \item Revise if predictors become non-significant
    \item Learn by doing—improve iteratively
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{8. TRANSPARENT COMMUNICATION}

\textbf{What I Say to Stakeholders:}

``These predictors are \textit{associated with} higher risk based on current evidence. We're confident the model improves targeting compared to random surveillance. However, we don't have perfect causal proof for every variable. We'll monitor model performance and update as we learn more. This is the best evidence-based approach available now, but we're committed to continuous improvement.''

\textbf{What I Don't Say:}

``This model is perfect and we know exactly why these variables cause outbreaks.''

\textbf{9. THE PHILOSOPHICAL POINT}

\textbf{All Models Are Wrong (George Box):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The question is: are they \textit{useful}?
    \item Perfect knowledge is impossible in complex systems
    \item We act on best available evidence, not certainty
    \item Science is iterative—we improve with new data
    \item Being ``less wrong'' than alternatives is sufficient for progress
\end{itemize}

\textbf{10. MY COMMITMENT}

\textbf{If new evidence shows I'm wrong:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item I will update the model
    \item I will publish corrections
    \item I will inform stakeholders
    \item I will not defend false conclusions
    \item Science requires changing our minds when data demand it
\end{itemize}

\textbf{The Honest Bottom Line:} I could be wrong about specific predictors, especially crop diversity and feed crops, which might be proxies for unmeasured factors. However, the model's overall predictive validity is robust, the approach is better than current practice, and we have safeguards to detect and correct errors. Uncertainty doesn't paralyze action—it motivates careful implementation, monitoring, and adaptive learning.''
