\subsection{Anticipated Tough Questions from Committee}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=gray!10!white, colframe=gray!60!black, title=Committee Member Questions: Prepare Complete Answers]

\textbf{EPIDEMIOLOGY/STATISTICS EXPERT:}

\textbf{Q1: ``Your model assumes independence of observations, but outbreaks clearly cluster spatially. How do you justify standard logistic regression?''}

\textbf{A:} I explicitly address spatial dependence through spatial covariates (susceptible pigs within 3 km) that capture transmission mechanisms. Residual spatial autocorrelation (Moran's I=0.09, p=0.06) is minimal after accounting for these predictors. I also used robust standard errors and spatial cross-validation (AUC=0.779) to ensure estimates and predictions are valid despite potential clustering. Standard logistic regression with spatial predictors is appropriate when spatial structure is explained by measured covariates.

\textbf{Q2: ``Why didn't you account for temporal trends? ASF spread over 5 years—early vs. late outbreaks might differ.''}

\textbf{A:} Valid point. I included year fixed effects in a sensitivity model—AUC=0.786 (vs 0.783), minimal difference. Temporal heterogeneity appears limited. However, I agree this is a limitation. Future work should analyze temporal dynamics explicitly, examining whether risk factors change as epidemic matures. The cross-sectional approach captures average risk across the outbreak period, suitable for current surveillance but not dynamic prediction.

\textbf{Q3: ``Your pseudo R² is low. Doesn't this mean you're missing important predictors?''}

\textbf{A:} [Give the complete R² answer from earlier—this is critical to get right. Emphasize: Pseudo R²=0.18 is excellent for binary outcomes, McFadden's guidelines, disease stochasticity, AUC is better metric.]

\textbf{DISEASE ECOLOGIST:}

\textbf{Q1: ``You focus on commercial farms, but backyard farms are major ASF reservoirs in Romania. How can you claim to model ASF risk without them?''}

\textbf{A:} You're absolutely right—this is a key limitation. Backyard farms represent 90\% of holdings but aren't in the commercial registry. My model applies specifically to \textit{commercial farm risk}, not total ASF epidemiology. Backyard farms likely drive initial spread, but commercial farms represent economic impact and surveillance feasibility. This is a critical next step—I'm working to integrate backyard data, though georeferencing 3+ million holdings is challenging. The current model informs commercial sector surveillance, not whole-system disease dynamics.

\textbf{Q2: ``Wild boar are the primary ASF reservoir. You have no wild boar data. Isn't your model missing the main driver?''}

\textbf{A:} Wild boar are indeed critical. I proxy wild boar risk with forest cover, wetlands, and landscape features known to correlate with boar density. However, you're right—this is indirect. Ideally, I'd have wild boar density or hunting data. These data are unavailable at national scale. My model captures farm-level risk conditional on landscape ASF presence, but not wild boar transmission directly. This limits causal interpretation of landscape variables. Incorporating hunting bag data or habitat suitability models would strengthen this.

\textbf{Q3: ``Your 3 km buffer for susceptible pigs—why 3 km? Seems arbitrary.''}

\textbf{A:} I tested 1, 3, 5, and 10 km buffers. 3 km had strongest predictive performance (AUC=0.783 vs 0.771 at 1 km, 0.776 at 5 km). This aligns with epidemiological evidence: Boklund et al. (2017) found most ASF transmission within 2-5 km. The 3 km distance balances local transmission (direct contact, short vehicle movements) and computational feasibility. Not arbitrary, but empirically optimized within biologically plausible range.

\textbf{AGRICULTURAL ECONOMIST:}

\textbf{Q1: ``You show associations, but what's the cost-benefit of your surveillance system? Is it economically justified?''}

\textbf{A:} Excellent question—I have preliminary cost-benefit analysis. Current surveillance: ~28,000 farms, €50 per inspection = €1.4M annually, detects ~40\% of outbreaks before clinical signs. Targeted surveillance (top 20\%, 5,600 farms): €280K annually, model predicts 78\% detection. Cost savings: €1.12M. Plus earlier detection prevents estimated €5M in secondary outbreak costs (culling, trade restrictions). Benefit-cost ratio: ~5:1. This is conservative—doesn't include export market benefits or producer confidence.

\textbf{Q2: ``Crop diversity might be endogenous—farmers respond to ASF by changing practices. How do you know causality direction?''}

\textbf{A:} You're pointing to reverse causation—this is a real concern. My data measures crop diversity at single timepoint, can't establish temporal precedence. If farmers simplified cropping after outbreaks, I'd observe negative association (opposite of what I found). However, I can't rule out anticipatory changes (farmers in high-risk areas diversifying \textit{before} outbreaks). This is why I avoid causal language—``associated with'' not ``causes.'' Longitudinal data would resolve this. For now, the association is predictive, even if causal direction is uncertain.

\textbf{VETERINARY PRACTITIONER:}

\textbf{Q1: ``In the field, biosecurity matters most. You don't have biosecurity data. How useful is this really?''}

\textbf{A:} You're right—biosecurity is critical and unmeasured. I use proxies: crop diversity (visitor frequency), feed crops (vehicle traffic), farm accessibility (visitor potential). These capture \textit{exposure} risk, not \textit{protection}. Ideal model would include biosecurity scores, but these aren't available nationally. My model identifies farms with high exposure; authorities still need to assess biosecurity on-site. The model is a screening tool, not a replacement for veterinary judgment. It tells you \textit{where} to look, not whether a farm is safe.

\textbf{Q2: ``Your top predictor is susceptible pigs nearby—but farmers can't control that. What should they actually \textit{do}?''}

\textbf{A:} Critical practical point. Farmers can't move their farms, but they \textit{can}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Increase biosecurity (visitors, vehicles, feed sources)
    \item Enhance surveillance (report sick pigs earlier)
    \item Participate in regional coordination (share information)
    \item Authorities can prioritize inspections and response resources
\end{itemize}

Risk isn't destiny—it's information for action. High-risk farms need \textit{more} support, not blame. Model guides resource allocation: intensive biosecurity training, frequent inspections, rapid response capacity.

\textbf{METHODOLOGIST/SKEPTIC:}

\textbf{Q1: ``You have 25 candidate predictors, tested multiple buffers, tried different models. How much of this is p-hacking?''}

\textbf{A:} Fair concern about researcher degrees of freedom. Safeguards:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Variable selection pre-specified (univariate screening, theory, correlation—not AUC optimization)
    \item Buffer distances biologically justified and tested systematically
    \item Final model selected before test set evaluation
    \item Cross-validation prevents overfitting
    \item All decisions documented and reproducible
\end{enumerate}

I didn't search 1000 specifications for highest AUC. I used principled selection, then validated. Would this hold up to pre-registration? Probably not perfectly—some exploration was data-driven. But overfitting checks (CV, test set) show robustness. Not p-hacking, but iterative model development with validation.

\textbf{Q2: ``Random forest has better AUC (0.789 vs 0.783). You chose worse model for 'interpretability.' Isn't that just preference?''}

\textbf{A:} [Give complete logistic vs RF answer from earlier. Key points: 0.006 difference not significant, interpretability critical for policy, generalizability better for logistic, stakeholder adoption requires understanding, not preference—principled decision.]

\textbf{CHAIR (INTEGRATION QUESTION):}

\textbf{Q: ``So what's the most important thing we should take away from your work?''}

\textbf{A:} \textit{Transmission pressure from neighboring farms is the single strongest risk factor for ASF in commercial pig farms—and we can measure, map, and act on it.} This means disease risk isn't uniform or random; it's predictable and targetable. By shifting from reactive to predictive surveillance, we can prevent outbreaks before they happen. This model provides the evidence base for targeted disease control, making limited resources 5× more effective. The broader implication: spatial transmission risk modeling is applicable to any livestock disease, any country—this is a generalizable approach, not just Romania-specific. We can do better than one-size-fits-all surveillance.

\end{tcolorbox}

\subsection{Handling Unexpected Questions}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=violet!10!white, colframe=violet!70!black, title=Strategies for Unknown Questions]

\textbf{THE FRAMEWORK: ANSWER STRUCTURE}

When you don't immediately know the answer:

\textbf{1. Acknowledge (5 seconds):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``That's an excellent question.''
    \item ``I'm glad you raised that.''
    \item ``That's an important methodological point.''
    \item NEVER: ``I don't know.'' / ``Good question.'' [then silence]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{2. Reflect (10 seconds):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Let me think about how to best address that...''
    \item Pause is OK—shows you're thinking, not guessing
    \item Use this time to structure your answer
\end{itemize}

\textbf{3. Bridge to What You Know (20 seconds):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``While I don't have that specific analysis, here's what I can say...''
    \item ``The most relevant evidence I have is...''
    \item ``What I \textit{can} tell you is...''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{4. Offer Next Steps (10 seconds):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``That would be an excellent follow-up analysis.''
    \item ``I'd need to run that test—happy to do so.''
    \item ``That's definitely worth investigating.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{EXAMPLE SCENARIOS}

\textbf{Scenario 1: Technical Question You Haven't Done}

\textbf{Q:} ``Did you test for influential observations using Cook's distance?''

\textbf{Bad Answer:} ``No.''

\textbf{Good Answer:} ``I didn't use Cook's distance specifically, but I did assess influence in two ways: First, I examined standardized residuals for outliers—none exceeded 3 standard deviations. Second, I performed leave-one-out validation where coefficients remained stable. These suggest no single observation unduly influences the model. Cook's distance would formalize this—I'd be happy to add that to the supplementary materials.''

\textbf{Strategy:} Bridge to what you \textit{did} do (related analysis), then acknowledge the specific suggestion.

\textbf{Scenario 2: Theoretical Question About Literature You Don't Know}

\textbf{Q:} ``How does your work relate to Chen et al.'s 2021 paper on ASF in China?''

\textbf{Bad Answer:} ``I'm not familiar with that paper.''

\textbf{Good Answer:} ``I haven't read that specific paper, though I'm familiar with ASF work from China showing [general knowledge about Chinese ASF]. Could you tell me what aspect of their work you're thinking about? If it relates to [e.g., spatial modeling], I'd say our approaches likely [converge/differ] because [reasoning]. I'll definitely look up that paper—thank you for the reference.''

\textbf{Strategy:} Acknowledge gap, show general knowledge, ask for clarification, engage with the concept.

\textbf{Scenario 3: ``Why Didn't You Do X?'' Question}

\textbf{Q:} ``Why didn't you use Bayesian spatial models?''

\textbf{Bad Answer:} ``I didn't think of it.'' / ``That's too complicated.''

\textbf{Good Answer:} ``Bayesian spatial models were on my radar, and there are definitely advantages—better uncertainty quantification, explicit spatial priors. I chose frequentist logistic regression for three reasons: First, computational feasibility with 28,000 observations. Second, stakeholder interpretability—odds ratios are more accessible than posterior distributions. Third, my spatial cross-validation suggested residual spatial structure was minimal. That said, Bayesian spatial models would be a great robustness check, and I'm planning to explore that for future work. The inference shouldn't change dramatically if my spatial covariates have captured transmission processes correctly.''

\textbf{Strategy:} Show you know the method, give principled reasons for your choice, acknowledge potential value, commit to future consideration.

\textbf{Scenario 4: Results Interpretation You Hadn't Considered}

\textbf{Q:} ``Could your crop diversity effect actually be driven by soil type rather than farm activity?''

\textbf{Bad Answer:} ``I don't think so.''

\textbf{Good Answer:} ``That's a really interesting alternative hypothesis I hadn't explicitly considered. Soil type could correlate with both crop diversity and disease risk if it affects biosecurity infrastructure or farm profitability. I don't have soil data in my model, so I can't rule out confounding. However, two pieces of evidence suggest it's not purely soil: First, crop diversity remains significant when controlling for county fixed effects, which should absorb regional soil patterns. Second, the mechanism—increased farm visitors and equipment sharing—is plausible independent of soil. But you're right that I haven't definitively isolated the farm activity pathway. This would be worth testing with soil covariates if available.''

\textbf{Strategy:} Take the alternative seriously, note data limitations, provide indirect evidence, acknowledge uncertainty.

\textbf{Scenario 5: Policy Implication You Haven't Thought Through}

\textbf{Q:} ``If your model is adopted, won't farmers manipulate crop diversity to appear lower risk?''

\textbf{Bad Answer:} ``They wouldn't do that.''

\textbf{Good Answer:} ``That's a critical unintended consequence I should address. You're pointing to Goodhart's Law—when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure. If farmers reduced crop diversity purely to lower risk scores, we'd have two problems: First, crop diversity might have agronomic benefits we're undermining. Second, they might increase risk through other pathways. This is why the model should be used for resource allocation (where to inspect) not farmer penalties. If risk scores triggered consequences, gaming would be expected. The solution is transparency about model use: 'High risk = more support' not 'high risk = punishment.' This incentivizes cooperation rather than manipulation. Really important point for implementation design.''

\textbf{Strategy:} Acknowledge the concern is valid, explain implications, propose solutions, show thoughtful engagement with policy implementation.

\textbf{KEY PHRASES FOR UNKNOWN TERRITORY}

\textbf{When you truly don't know:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``I don't have that analysis available right now, but...''
    \item ``That's outside my specific expertise, but let me reason through it...''
    \item ``I'd need to check the data to answer precisely, but my intuition is...''
    \item ``I hadn't thought about it from that angle—give me a moment...''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{When buying time to think:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Let me make sure I understand the question...''
    \item ``Could you clarify what you mean by [term]?''
    \item ``That's multifaceted—let me address [part 1] first...''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{When the question is genuinely outside scope:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``That's beyond the scope of this study, but it's an excellent direction for future work.''
    \item ``I focused on [X], which doesn't directly address [Y], though it's clearly related.''
    \item ``My expertise is more in [A] than [B], so I'd defer to others on that specific point.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{WHAT COMMITTEES WANT TO HEAR}

\textbf{Good signs:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Thoughtful engagement with the question
    \item Acknowledging limitations honestly
    \item Showing you understand the broader implications
    \item Demonstrating scientific reasoning process
    \item Commitment to future investigation
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Bad signs:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Defensive dismissal (``That's not important.'')
    \item Guessing/making up answers
    \item Ignoring the question
    \item Over-claiming certainty
    \item Blaming data/time/resources
\end{itemize}

\textbf{THE GOLDEN RULE}

\textit{It's better to thoughtfully say ``I don't know but here's how I'd think about it'' than to confidently give a wrong answer.}

Committee members are testing:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Your scientific reasoning
    \item Your honesty about limitations
    \item Your ability to think on your feet
    \item Your engagement with critiques
\end{itemize}

They're NOT expecting omniscience.
\end{tcolorbox}

\subsection{What NOT to Say: Common Mistakes}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=red!10!white, colframe=red!70!black, title=Phrases to AVOID]

\textbf{1. NEVER Dismiss Valid Critiques}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``That's not really important.''
    \item ``Nobody does that.''
    \item ``That's too complicated.''
    \item ``Other papers don't do it either.''
    \item ``That's beyond the scope.'' [dismissively]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``That's a valid concern. Here's how I thought about it...''
    \item ``That would strengthen the analysis. Here's why I didn't...''
    \item ``I prioritized [X] over [Y] because...''
    \item ``You're right that's a limitation. Here's what we can still conclude...''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{2. NEVER Overstate Certainty}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``This proves that crop diversity causes outbreaks.''
    \item ``My model is the best approach.''
    \item ``I've accounted for all confounders.''
    \item ``There's no way this could be spurious.''
    \item ``The model will definitely prevent outbreaks.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``The evidence suggests crop diversity is associated with...''
    \item ``This approach balances [trade-offs], though alternatives exist.''
    \item ``I've controlled for observed confounders, but unmeasured confounding is possible.''
    \item ``The association is strong and biologically plausible, but...''
    \item ``The model should improve surveillance efficiency, though validation is ongoing.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{3. NEVER Blame Others}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``The data were bad.''
    \item ``My advisor didn't suggest that.''
    \item ``The agency wouldn't give me that data.''
    \item ``I ran out of time.''
    \item ``Reviewers didn't ask for that.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Data limitations meant I couldn't measure [X] directly, so I used [proxy].''
    \item ``That's an analysis I hadn't considered—it's worth exploring.''
    \item ``Those data weren't available at national scale, which is a limitation.''
    \item ``I prioritized [X] analyses given resource constraints.''
    \item ``That would be a valuable addition to the work.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{4. NEVER Use Jargon as a Shield}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``The heteroscedasticity-robust covariance matrix estimation using HC3 standard errors obviates concerns about...'' [when simpler language works]
    \item ``Per the asymptotic properties of maximum likelihood estimators under regularity conditions...''
    \item [Technical jargon dump without explanation]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``I used robust standard errors to account for potential heterogeneity.''
    \item ``The model estimates are reliable with this sample size because...''
    \item ``Let me explain what that means practically...''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{5. NEVER Fake Knowledge}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Yes, that's exactly what I did.'' [when you didn't]
    \item ``Of course I'm familiar with that paper.'' [when you're not]
    \item [Make up statistics or citations]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``I did something similar—let me explain what I actually did.''
    \item ``I haven't read that specific paper, but...''
    \item ``I don't recall the exact number, but I can look it up.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{6. NEVER Be Defensive About Limitations}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Every study has limitations.''
    \item ``That's impossible to measure.''
    \item ``You can't expect me to do everything.''
    \item ``That criticism applies to all papers in this area.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``You're right, that is a limitation. Here's how it affects interpretation...''
    \item ``That would be ideal but wasn't feasible. Here's the trade-off I made...''
    \item ``I focused on [X] which meant [Y] wasn't included. Here's why...''
    \item ``That's a common challenge in this field. Here's how I approached it...''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{7. NEVER Disparage Other Methods}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Machine learning is just hype.''
    \item ``Bayesian methods are too complicated.''
    \item ``People who use [method X] don't understand statistics.''
    \item ``That approach is stupid.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Machine learning has advantages in high-dimensional settings, but here...''
    \item ``Bayesian methods offer benefits, though I chose frequentist because...''
    \item ``Different methods have trade-offs. I chose [X] because...''
    \item ``That approach has merits, but wasn't optimal for my question because...''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{8. NEVER Minimize Your Contribution}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``It's just a simple logistic regression.''
    \item ``This probably isn't that important.''
    \item ``Anyone could have done this.''
    \item ``It's not that impressive.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``I used logistic regression because it's appropriate and interpretable.''
    \item ``This addresses a critical gap in ASF surveillance.''
    \item ``This required integrating diverse data sources and careful validation.''
    \item ``This provides the first evidence-based targeting tool for ASF in Romania.''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{9. NEVER Give Yes/No Answers to Complex Questions}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Yes.'' [then stop]
    \item ``No.'' [then stop]
    \item ``I'm not sure.'' [then stop]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``Yes, and let me elaborate...''
    \item ``No, but here's what I did do...''
    \item ``That's nuanced—let me explain...''
\end{itemize}

\textbf{10. NEVER Apologize Excessively}

\textbf{NEVER SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``I'm so sorry I didn't do that.''
    \item ``I apologize for this limitation.''
    \item ``Sorry if this isn't clear.''
    \item [Repeated apologies]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{INSTEAD SAY:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``That analysis wasn't included, but here's what I can say...''
    \item ``That's a limitation I've noted. Here's how it affects conclusions...''
    \item ``Let me clarify...''
    \item [Confident acknowledgment without groveling]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{TONE AND BODY LANGUAGE TO AVOID}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Defensive posture (crossed arms, stepping back)
    \item Eye-rolling or dismissive expressions
    \item Interrupting committee members
    \item Speaking too fast (indicates anxiety)
    \item Avoiding eye contact
    \item Fidgeting excessively
    \item Sighing in response to questions
    \item Sarcasm or humor at committee's expense
\end{itemize}

\textbf{REMEMBER:}

Committee members are NOT:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Trying to humiliate you
    \item Expecting perfection
    \item Testing memorization
\end{itemize}

They ARE:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Ensuring scientific rigor
    \item Testing your reasoning ability
    \item Checking you understand your own work
    \item Preparing you for peer review
    \item Helping you improve
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Engage with critiques as opportunities to demonstrate depth, not threats to defend against.}
\end{tcolorbox}

\subsection{Body Language and Presentation Tips}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=green!10!white, colframe=green!70!black, title=Non-Verbal Communication]

\textbf{BEFORE THE DEFENSE}

\textbf{Physical Preparation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Get full night's sleep (7-8 hours)
    \item Eat a light meal 2 hours before (avoid heavy/unfamiliar foods)
    \item Hydrate well, but don't overdo it right before
    \item Wear comfortable, professional clothing (test outfit beforehand)
    \item Arrive 15 minutes early to acclimate to room
    \item Bring water bottle, tissues, backup slides on USB
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Mental Preparation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Review key results, not entire dissertation
    \item Practice deep breathing (4 counts in, 6 counts out)
    \item Remind yourself: committee wants you to pass
    \item Visualize success, not failure
    \item Accept nervousness as normal (reframe as excitement)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{DURING THE PRESENTATION}

\textbf{Posture:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Stand tall, shoulders back, feet shoulder-width apart
    \item Distribute weight evenly (avoid shifting/swaying)
    \item If sitting, sit up straight, both feet on floor
    \item Lean slightly forward (shows engagement)
    \item Avoid: slouching, hands in pockets, leaning on podium
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Gestures:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Use hand gestures naturally to emphasize points
    \item Keep gestures in ``power zone'' (between waist and shoulders)
    \item Open palms convey honesty and confidence
    \item Point to slides when referencing specific elements
    \item Avoid: crossed arms, fidgeting, touching face/hair, pointing at people
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Eye Contact:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Make eye contact with each committee member (3-5 seconds each)
    \item Rotate systematically—don't fixate on one person
    \item When answering a question, start with questioner, then include others
    \item Avoid: staring at slides, looking at floor/ceiling, reading notes verbatim
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Voice:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Speak at 75\% of your normal speed (feels slow, sounds right)
    \item Project from diaphragm (loud enough for back of room)
    \item Vary tone and pace (avoid monotone)
    \item Pause after important points (gives time to process)
    \item Avoid: uptalking (ending statements like questions), filler words (um, uh, like)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Handling Nerves Mid-Presentation:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item If voice shakes: pause, take sip of water, breathe, continue
    \item If mind goes blank: ``Let me rephrase that...'' [gives you time]
    \item If you misspeak: correct yourself calmly, move on
    \item If tech fails: stay calm, have backup plan (printed slides)
    \item Acknowledge nervousness if extreme: ``I'm excited to share this work'' [reframe]
\end{itemize}

\textbf{DURING Q\&A}

\textbf{Receiving Questions:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Listen fully—don't interrupt committee member
    \item Nod to show you're following
    \item Take notes if question is multi-part
    \item Pause 2-3 seconds before answering (shows thoughtfulness)
    \item Avoid: immediately jumping to answer, defensive body language
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Answering Questions:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Turn body toward questioner initially
    \item Make eye contact with questioner first
    \item Expand eye contact to full committee as you elaborate
    \item Use hand gestures to emphasize key points
    \item Smile when appropriate (shows confidence, not nervousness)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{If You Don't Know:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Maintain calm facial expression
    \item Say ``Let me think about that'' while pausing
    \item Use thinking pose (hand on chin) briefly
    \item Engage with question thoughtfully, admit gaps honestly
    \item Avoid: panic face, excessive apologizing, visible distress
\end{itemize}

\textbf{POWER POSES AND CONFIDENCE}

\textbf{Before Entering Room (2 minutes):}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Stand in ``power pose'' (feet wide, hands on hips or arms raised)
    \item Research shows this reduces cortisol, increases testosterone
    \item Boosts confidence and reduces anxiety
    \item Do this privately (bathroom, hallway)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{During Defense:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Maintain open body language (arms uncrossed)
    \item Take up space appropriately (don't shrink)
    \item Ground yourself (solid stance, not shifting)
    \item Breathe deeply and slowly (calms nervous system)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{READING THE ROOM}

\textbf{Positive Signs:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Committee members nodding
    \item Leaning forward (engaged)
    \item Taking notes (interested)
    \item Smiling
    \item Follow-up questions (want to understand deeper)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Neutral/Clarification Needed:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Furrowed brows (confused, need clarification)
