\subsection{Practice Scenarios with Model Answers}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!10!white, colframe=blue!70!black, title=Role-Play Exercises: Simulate These]

\textbf{SCENARIO 1: The Methodological Barrage}

\textit{Committee member asks 3 rapid-fire questions without pausing:}

\textbf{Questions:}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item ``Did you test for spatial autocorrelation in model residuals?''
    \item ``What about temporal heterogeneity across the 5-year outbreak period?''
    \item ``And how do you know your results aren't driven by a few influential observations?''
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{MODEL ANSWER:}

[Write down each question while they're speaking]

``Let me address each of those in turn—all excellent methodological points.

\textbf{First, spatial autocorrelation:} Yes, I tested Moran's I on model residuals. The value was 0.09 with p=0.06—no significant residual clustering after accounting for spatial predictors. This suggests the susceptible pig density variable adequately captures spatial transmission processes.

\textbf{Second, temporal heterogeneity:} I ran a sensitivity analysis with year fixed effects. The AUC changed minimally—0.786 versus 0.783. Risk factors appear stable across the outbreak period, though I agree a more sophisticated temporal analysis would be valuable for future work, especially for dynamic predictions.

\textbf{Third, influential observations:} I examined standardized residuals—none exceeded 3 standard deviations—and performed leave-one-out validation where coefficients remained stable. I didn't formally use Cook's distance, but these diagnostics suggest no single farm drives the results.

Did I cover everything, or would you like me to elaborate on any of those?''

\textbf{KEY TACTICS:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Write down questions (shows you're organized)
    \item Signal structure (``Let me address each in turn'')
    \item Answer each directly
    \item Acknowledge gaps honestly (``I didn't formally use Cook's distance'')
    \item Check back (``Did I cover everything?'')
\end{itemize}

\textbf{SCENARIO 2: The ``You're Wrong'' Challenge}

\textit{Committee member directly challenges your interpretation:}

\textbf{Question:} ``I think you're wrong about crop diversity. It's not farm visitors—it's probably just that diverse farms are in higher-risk regions. You haven't ruled out spatial confounding.''

\textbf{MODEL ANSWER:}

``That's a really important alternative hypothesis, and you're right that spatial confounding is a concern. Let me explain why I think farm-level mechanisms are at play, while acknowledging this isn't definitive.

\textbf{Evidence against pure spatial confounding:}

\textbf{First,} I included county fixed effects in a sensitivity model—crop diversity remained significant (OR=1.49, p<0.001). This controls for all time-invariant regional characteristics, including base disease risk.

\textbf{Second,} the effect persists after controlling for outbreak density within 3 km, which should capture local disease clustering.

\textbf{Third,} the mechanism is biologically plausible—literature documents increased vehicle and visitor traffic on diversified farms.

\textbf{However,} you're absolutely right that I can't definitively rule out unmeasured regional confounders—maybe crop diversity correlates with agricultural policies, infrastructure, or practices I haven't measured.

\textbf{The honest answer:} I've done what I can with observational data to address spatial confounding, but I can't claim perfect isolation of the farm-level effect. Longitudinal data—tracking farms that change crop diversity over time—would be the gold standard. This is an important limitation that affects causal interpretation but doesn't invalidate the predictive utility.

What specific spatial factors did you have in mind? I'd be interested in your thoughts on what might be driving this.''

